# Drift Detection: Terminology & Architecture

Spec: DriftDetection
Purpose:
  Define terminology drift detection (when codebase terms diverge from
  project policy terms), architecture drift detection (when code violates
  declared architecture), violation lifecycle, accepted violations,
  and time-limited architecture exceptions.

Owns: lifecycle/drift.sdd

## Terminology Drift

  When the project policy defines preferred terms but the codebase uses
  different terms, this is terminology drift.

  Project policy:
    Use: Handler, Service, Repository

  Codebase:
    Manager, Processor, Provider

  AI creates:
    @observation.terminology-drift

  Format:
    @observation.term-O1

    type: terminology_drift
    policy_terms:
      - Handler
      - Service
      - Repository
    observed_terms:
      - Manager
      - Processor
      - Provider
    mapping:
      Manager → Handler
      Processor → Service
      Provider → Repository
    confidence: MEDIUM
    severity: LOW

  Rules:
    [TERM-01] Terminology drift is an OBSERVATION, not a finding.
              AI MUST NOT change code to match policy terms without human approval.
    [TERM-02] AI creates @observation.terminology-drift before any
              recommendation to rename.
    [TERM-03] Terminology drift observations are linked to the canonical
              @concept.* that the policy term maps to.

## Architecture Drift

  When the declared architecture in .sdd/ differs from the actual
  source code implementation.

  Declared (.sdd):
    @architecture.ddd
      Payment → Domain → Application → Infrastructure

  Observed (code):
    PaymentService → directly calls → PostgreSQL

  AI creates:
    @violation.V22

  Format:
    @violation.V22

    principle: @architecture.ddd
    found_in: payment/service.go
    reason: PaymentService directly accesses PostgreSQL, bypassing domain layer
    severity: HIGH
    status: DETECTED
    detected_at: 2026-09-01T14:30:00Z

  Drift Classification:
    VALID_DRIFT:       intentional and documented (has @exception.* or @decision.*)
    UNEXPLAINED_DRIFT: change without corresponding task/decision
    UNTRACEABLE_CHANGE: source changed with no task, decision, or workflow

  Rules:
    [DRIFT-01] Graph MUST detect all drift types (per graph/model.sdd).
    [DRIFT-02] Drift MUST be classified as VALID or UNEXPLAINED.
    [DRIFT-03] Untraceable changes MUST be flagged.
    [DRIFT-04] Drift MUST NOT be silently ignored.

## Violation Lifecycle

  DETECTED
    ↓
  REVIEWED
    ↓
  ┌──────────────┬──────────────┐
  ACCEPTED      FALSE_POSITIVE
    ↓              ↓
  FIXED          (rejected)
    ↓
  VERIFIED

  DETECTED:
    description: Violation found by drift detection or code analysis
    action: status set to DETECTED, notify reviewer

  REVIEWED:
    description: A human or automated gate has examined the violation
    action: status set to REVIEWED, outcome pending

  ACCEPTED:
    description: Violation is valid and should be fixed
    action: generate @task.* to fix, or @exception.* if intentional
    rule: [VIOL-01] Accepted violations MUST have a planned fix or exception.

  FALSE_POSITIVE:
    description: Violation is a false alarm
    action: mark FALSE_POSITIVE, archive

  FIXED:
    description: Code or architecture has been corrected
    action: status set to FIXED

  VERIFIED:
    description: Fix confirmed by re-running drift detection
    action: status set to VERIFIED, close violation

  Rules:
    [VIOL-02] Violations MUST be reviewed within 48 hours of detection.
    [VIOL-03] Unreviewed violations older than 7 days trigger escalation.
    [VIOL-04] FIXED violations MUST be re-verified by drift detection.

## Accepted Violations

  Sometimes a violation is knowingly accepted (for performance, legacy,
  or practical reasons).

  @violation.V22
    severity: HIGH
    status: ACCEPTED
    accepted_by: @decision.D91
    accepted_reason: "PaymentService is performance-critical; domain
                      layer adds unacceptable latency in benchmark tests"

  Rules:
    [ACPT-01] Accepted violations MUST reference a @decision.* record.
    [ACPT-02] AI NEVER flags an accepted violation as an error.
    [ACPT-03] Accepted violations are re-evaluated annually.

## Architecture Exceptions

  @exception.E22

  rule:
    @architecture.ddd (domain cannot access infrastructure)

  exception:
    RefundProviderAdapter

  reason:
    legacy integration with external provider

  expires:
    2026-12-01

  approved_by:
    @human

  status:
    ACTIVE

  Exception Lifecycle:
    ACTIVE → EXPIRED (on expiry date) → REVOKED (if revoked)

  Rules:
    [EXC-01] Every exception MUST have an expiry date (max 1 year).
    [EXC-02] AI MUST flag EXCEPTION_EXPIRED when expiry date passes.
    [EXC-03] Expired exceptions revert their violations to DETECTED status.
    [EXC-04] Exception extensions require a new @decision.* approval.

  Example expiry handling:
    On 2026-12-02 (day after expiry):
    @exception.E22  →  status: EXPIRED
    @violation.V22   →  status: DETECTED (was ACCEPTED via exception)
    AI alerts: "Architecture exception E22 has expired; violation V22
                is now active again. Extension requires new approval."

## Drift Types (Extended)

  STRUCTURAL
    Declared directory/module layout diverges from code.
    Example:
      .sdd:  internal/payment/domain
      code:  internal/payment/core

  BEHAVIORAL
    Declared behavior differs from runtime behavior.
    Example:
      .sdd:  refund is idempotent
      code:  duplicate request → duplicate refund

  CONTRACT
    API/interface contract diverges from implementation.
    Example:
      .sdd:  POST /payments/refund → { "id": "..." }
      code:  POST /payments/refund → { "refund_id": "..." }

  DATABASE
    Schema/constraint in .sdd diverges from actual DB/migration.
    Example:
      .sdd:  users.email NOT NULL UNIQUE
      code:  email nullable

  SECURITY
    Security policy in .sdd diverges from implementation/runtime.
    Example:
      .sdd:  secrets must never enter AI context
      code:  secret injected into prompt at runtime

  CONFIGURATION
    Declared config requirement diverges from deployed/runtime config.
    Example:
      .sdd:  Redis required
      deploy: REDIS_ENABLED=false

  DOCUMENTATION
    Code/db/runtime surface diverges from docs.
    Example:
      .sdd/docs:  POST /v2/refunds
      code:        POST /v1/refunds

  TERMINOLOGY
    Codebase terms diverge from policy terms.
    (Existing terminology_drift section above)

## Drift Classification

  DECLARATION_WRONG:   .sdd is outdated; code follows newer requirement
  CODE_WRONG:          .sdd is correct; code violates declared rule
  BOTH_OUTDATED:       Neither .sdd nor code reflects current requirement; redesign needed
  UNKNOWN:             Insufficient evidence to classify

## Source-of-Truth Matrix

  @truth.<domain>
    source: <sdd | code | migrations | openapi | runtime-observation | human>
    authority: <who/what decides>

  Examples:
    @truth.api
      source: openapi

    @truth.database
      source: migrations

    @truth.runtime
      source: runtime-observation

    @truth.architecture
      source: sdd

## Epistemic States

  OBSERVED:    Fact directly seen in code/runtime/DB/infra
  INFERRED:    Conclusion drawn from observations with reasoning chain
  DECLARED:    Fact asserted in .sdd (may be outdated)
  VERIFIED:    Fact confirmed by proof (test, static analysis, runtime check)

## Sync Operations

  SYNC_SDD:    Code is correct, .sdd is outdated → update .sdd
  SYNC_CODE:   .sdd is correct, code violates → fix code
  SYNC_BOTH:   New requirement invalidates both → update both
  NO_CHANGE:   Drift is accepted (has @exception.* or @decision.*)
  REVIEW:      Insufficient evidence to auto-sync

## Evidence System

  Drift record MUST include:
    evidence:
      - @evidence.E<id>
      - @test.T<id>
      - @code.C<id>
      - @decision.D<id>

  Evidence sources:
    STATIC      — static analysis, AST, grep
    TEST        — test suite result
    RUNTIME     — runtime observation, profiler, log
    HUMAN       — human review, approval
    EXTERNAL    — third-party tool, API response
    FORMAL      — formal verification, proof assistant

## Proof & Confidence

  @proof.P<id>
    rule:        <@architecture.rule.R<id>>
    type:        <STATIC | TEST | RUNTIME | HUMAN | EXTERNAL | FORMAL>
    result:      <PASS | FAIL | PARTIAL>
    confidence:  <HIGH | MEDIUM | LOW>
    evidence:    [<ids>]

  Confidence matrix:
    HIGH   — multiple evidence types, verified by tests/runtime
    MEDIUM — single evidence type, static analysis only
    LOW    — inference only, no direct evidence

## No Silent Sync

  Any architecture rule change MUST produce:
    @decision.D<id>
    from: <old>
    to:   <new>
    reason: <why>
    impact: [<affected domains>]
    migration: [<task ids>]

    lineage:
      <old architecture>
        ↓
      D<id>
        ↓
      <new architecture>
        ↓
      T<task>
        ↓
      code
        ↓
      tests
        ↓
      verification

## Drift Integration

  Drift detection runs:
    - After task completion (per learning-gate.sdd)
    - Weekly (per health-score.sdd)
    - On demand (via sdd-health command with --drift flag, /sdd-drift command)

  Detected drift flows:
    terminology_drift → @observation.terminology-drift → reviewed by compactor
    architecture_drift → @violation.* → violation lifecycle
    structural_drift → @drift.D<id> → sync proposal
    behavioral_drift → @drift.D<id> → classification → sync task
    contract_drift → @drift.D<id> → sync proposal
    database_drift → @drift.D<id> → migration task
    security_drift → @drift.D<id> → security event
    configuration_drift → @drift.D<id> → config update
    documentation_drift → @drift.D<id> → doc sync

